{
"_about": "Kirsopp Lake’s footnotes to his Greek text (G the common ancestor of the Greek manuscripts; L the Latin version; Eus. Eusebius), and in \"refs\" the passages of Scripture he prints in the margin (with the digital file’s own reference, \"urn\"). The digital file has only the Greek pages, so the footnotes Lake prints under his English translation on the facing pages are not here. \"at\" is the verse the note or reference stands in (\"title\": the title above the greeting). Greek inside a note is in Beta Code, as in the digital file (so h(mw=n = ἡμῶν), and the file has slips in the notes’ English and Greek. From First1KGreek (Open Greek and Latin), CC BY-SA 4.0.",
"notes": [
{
"at": "4:2",
"note": "The MSS read h(mw=n your, but the confusion betseen u(mw=n and h(mw=n is so common that our may safely be restored."
},
{
"at": "9:1",
"note": "tw=| lo/gw| th=s dikaiosu/nhs GL, om, Eus."
},
{
"at": "9:2",
"note": "Here G breaks off, but the rest of the sentence is given by L Eus."
},
{
"at": "11:1",
"note": "An et after casti sould be natural, but is is only found in tso of the MSS of L."
},
{
"at": "11:3",
"note": "Some MSS. of L read deum instead of dominum."
},
{
"at": "12:2",
"note": "Et deum is omitted by some of the MSS of L."
},
{
"at": "13:1",
"note": "The greek is here again available from the #3uotation in eusebius."
},
{
"at": "13:1",
"note": "pe/mpw Eus. misero (=pe/myw) L"
}
],
